sections.s2_technical:
Scattered null checks are a symptom, not a fix. Each if-not-null adds a branch, obscures the real logic, and still leaves gaps because you cannot guard every call site, so NullPointerExceptions keep appearing. The senior response is to make null rare by design and handle it in a few deliberate places.

The first technique is fail-fast validation at the boundary. At construction and at public method entry, reject null with Objects.requireNonNull, so an invalid value is caught immediately with a clear message:

```java
public Account(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
    this.accountId = java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(accountId, "accountId required");
    this.balance   = java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(balance, "balance required");
}
```

Because the constructor enforces this invariant, every method of Account can use accountId without a null check.

The second technique is to return empty, not null, from APIs. A method that returns a list should return an empty list when there is nothing, and a method that may have no single result should return Optional, so callers never receive a null to guard:

```java
public List<Trade> findTrades(String accountId) { return results == null ? List.of() : results; }
public Optional<Account> findAccount(String id) { return Optional.ofNullable(lookup(id)); }
```

The third technique is the Null Object pattern, which replaces a null check with polymorphism. Instead of returning null for a missing customer and checking for it everywhere, you return a do-nothing implementation, like a GuestUser, whose methods behave sensibly, so callers just call methods without branching.

The fourth technique is to keep Optional for return values only, not for fields or method parameters, because Optional fields complicate serialization and Optional parameters are awkward; for absence in parameters you use overloads or sensible defaults instead.

A senior point is where null handling concentrates: validate inputs at the edges, guarantee non-null invariants in constructors, and express absence through empty collections, Optional returns, or Null Objects. Interior business logic then assumes non-null and reads cleanly. Another senior point is that this is not about never writing a null check; it is about writing a few intentional ones at boundaries instead of hundreds of defensive ones scattered through the core.

